INSERT INTO topic_sections (topic_id, title, content, section_type, order_index) VALUES
('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
'Concept Overview: What is Algebra?',
E'## What is Algebra and Why Does It Matter?\n\nAlgebra is the branch of mathematics that replaces specific numbers with **letters (called variables)** to express general mathematical truths. Instead of saying "3 plus something equals 10", we write `3 + x = 10` and solve for x.\n\nAlgebra is not just an abstract exercise — it is the language of every scientific, engineering, financial, and technological field in the world. When engineers design bridges, when economists model markets, when pharmacists calculate drug dosages — they use algebra.\n\n**In WASSCE Mathematics, algebra covers these key areas:**\n\n- Solving **linear equations** (highest power = 1)\n- Solving **quadratic equations** (highest power = 2)\n- Working with **simultaneous equations** (two equations, two unknowns solved together)\n- Solving **linear and quadratic inequalities**\n- Simplifying and manipulating **algebraic expressions**\n- **Word problems** that require setting up equations from real-life situations\n\n**Exam Importance:** Algebra constitutes 25-35% of all WASSCE Mathematics marks. Every section of the exam — from multiple choice to theory to the calculation paper — tests algebraic reasoning. A student who masters algebra has a massive advantage.\n\n**What you will be able to do after this topic:**\n- Solve any linear equation, no matter how complex\n- Solve quadratic equations using three different methods\n- Find the solution to any pair of simultaneous equations\n- Solve linear inequalities and represent answers on a number line\n- Translate real-world problems into equations and solve them',
'overview', 1),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
'Definitions & Key Terminologies',
E'## Every Term You Need to Know\n\n### Variables and Constants\n\n**Variable**: A letter that represents an unknown or changing quantity. Common choices are x, y, z, a, b, n.\n- Example: In `5x + 3 = 18`, x is the variable.\n\n**Constant**: A fixed numerical value that does not change.\n- Example: In `5x + 3`, the constant is 3.\n\n**Coefficient**: The number directly multiplying a variable.\n- Example: In `7x`, the coefficient is 7. In `-3y`, the coefficient is -3.\n\n### Expressions, Equations, and Identities\n\n**Algebraic Expression**: A mathematical phrase combining variables, constants, and operations (no equals sign).\n- Examples: `2x + 5`, `x² - 3x + 7`, `(a + b)/2`\n\n**Equation**: A mathematical statement asserting that two expressions are equal (has an = sign).\n- Examples: `2x + 5 = 11`, `x² - 3x - 10 = 0`\n\n**Identity**: An equation that is TRUE for ALL values of the variable.\n- Example: `(a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b²` is always true.\n\n**Formula**: An equation expressing a relationship between quantities.\n- Example: `A = πr²` (area of circle)\n\n### Types of Equations\n\n**Linear Equation**: The variable appears only to the power of 1. Graph is a straight line.\n- Standard form: `ax + b = c` where a ≠ 0\n- Example: `3x - 7 = 14`\n\n**Quadratic Equation**: The variable appears to the power of 2. Graph is a parabola.\n- Standard form: `ax² + bx + c = 0` where a ≠ 0\n- Example: `x² - 5x + 6 = 0`\n\n**Simultaneous Equations**: A system of two or more equations with two or more unknowns.\n- Example: `2x + y = 7` and `x - y = 2` — solved together.\n\n### Inequality Symbols\n\n| Symbol | Meaning | Example |\n|--------|---------|----------|\n| > | Greater than | x > 5 means x is more than 5 |\n| < | Less than | x < 3 means x is less than 3 |\n| >= | Greater than or equal | x >= 2 means x is 2 or more |\n| <= | Less than or equal | x <= 7 means x is 7 or less |\n| ≠ | Not equal to | x ≠ 0 means x is not zero |\n\n### Solution and Roots\n\n**Solution**: The value(s) of the variable that make an equation true.\n- For `2x + 5 = 11`: x = 3 is the solution (since 2(3)+5 = 11 ✓)\n\n**Root**: Used particularly for quadratic equations — the values of x that make the equation equal zero.\n- `x² - 5x + 6 = 0` has roots x = 2 and x = 3.\n\n**Verification/Check**: Substituting your answer back into the original equation to confirm it is correct. ALWAYS do this in WASSCE exams.',
'definitions', 2),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
'Deep Explanation: Linear Equations, Quadratics & Simultaneous Equations',
E'## PART 1: Solving Linear Equations\n\n### The Fundamental Principle\n\nThe golden rule of algebra: **Whatever operation you perform on one side of an equation, you MUST perform the exact same operation on the other side.**\n\nThis maintains the "balance" of the equation — like a perfectly balanced scale.\n\n### Step-by-Step Method for Linear Equations\n\n**Step 1: Expand all brackets** (using the distributive law)\n**Step 2: Clear any fractions** (multiply every term by the LCM of denominators)\n**Step 3: Collect like terms** (variables on one side, numbers on the other)\n**Step 4: Divide to find the variable**\n**Step 5: Verify your answer**\n\n### Expanding Brackets (Distributive Law)\n\n`a(b + c) = ab + ac`\n`a(b - c) = ab - ac`\n\nThis means you multiply the term OUTSIDE the brackets by EVERY term inside.\n\n**Common error**: Students often multiply only the first term inside.\n- WRONG: `3(x + 4) = 3x + 4`\n- CORRECT: `3(x + 4) = 3x + 12`\n\n### Clearing Fractions\n\nIf your equation contains fractions, multiply EVERY term on BOTH sides by the LCM (Lowest Common Multiple) of all denominators. This eliminates all fractions.\n\n---\n\n## PART 2: Solving Quadratic Equations\n\nA quadratic equation has the form **ax² + bx + c = 0** where a ≠ 0.\n\nThere are three methods. Learn all three — different questions demand different methods.\n\n### Method 1: Factorisation\n\nThis works when the quadratic can be factored into two linear brackets.\n\n**For equations where a = 1** (x² + bx + c = 0):\nFind two numbers p and q such that: p × q = c AND p + q = b\nThen write: (x + p)(x + q) = 0\n\n**For equations where a ≠ 1** (ax² + bx + c = 0):\nFind two numbers p and q such that: p × q = a × c AND p + q = b\nSplit the middle term and factor in pairs.\n\n**Zero Product Property**: If (x + p)(x + q) = 0, then EITHER x + p = 0 OR x + q = 0.\n\n### Method 2: The Quadratic Formula (Most Reliable)\n\nFor any quadratic ax² + bx + c = 0:\n\n**x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / (2a)**\n\nThe ± symbol means you calculate TWO answers:\n- x = (-b + √(b² - 4ac)) / (2a) ... first root\n- x = (-b - √(b² - 4ac)) / (2a) ... second root\n\n**The Discriminant** D = b² - 4ac tells you the nature of roots:\n- D > 0: Two distinct real roots\n- D = 0: One repeated root (both roots are equal)\n- D < 0: No real roots (roots are imaginary)\n\n### Method 3: Completing the Square\n\nThis transforms ax² + bx + c into the form a(x + h)² + k.\n\n**Steps for x² + bx + c = 0:**\n1. Move c to the right: x² + bx = -c\n2. Add (b/2)² to BOTH sides: x² + bx + (b/2)² = -c + (b/2)²\n3. Left side is now a perfect square: (x + b/2)² = -c + (b/2)²\n4. Take square root of both sides (remember ±)\n5. Solve for x\n\n---\n\n## PART 3: Simultaneous Equations\n\nSimultaneous equations are solved TOGETHER because together they have a unique solution.\n\n### Elimination Method\n\n**Goal**: Make the coefficient of one variable the same in both equations, then add or subtract to eliminate it.\n\n**Steps:**\n1. Label the equations (1) and (2)\n2. Multiply one or both equations by suitable numbers to make the coefficient of one variable equal\n3. Add equations if the equal coefficients have OPPOSITE signs; subtract if they have the SAME sign\n4. Solve the resulting single-variable equation\n5. Substitute back to find the second variable\n6. Verify in BOTH original equations\n\n### Substitution Method\n\n**Steps:**\n1. From one equation, express one variable in terms of the other (choose the simpler-looking equation)\n2. Substitute this expression into the OTHER equation\n3. Solve the resulting equation\n4. Substitute back to find the other variable\n\n---\n\n## PART 4: Linear Inequalities\n\n### The Critical Rule\n\nSolve inequalities EXACTLY like linear equations, with ONE exception:\n\n**When you multiply or divide BOTH sides by a NEGATIVE number, REVERSE the inequality sign.**\n\n- If `-2x > 8`, divide by -2: `x < -4` (sign reversed!)\n\n### Number Line Representation\n\nAlways draw a number line to show the solution:\n- **Open circle** ○ at a value means that value is NOT included (strict inequality: < or >)\n- **Closed circle** ● at a value means that value IS included (≤ or ≥)\n- Arrow pointing right: values going to the right (greater)\n- Arrow pointing left: values going to the left (smaller)\n\n### Combined Inequalities\n\nSome questions give TWO inequalities that must both be satisfied.\nFind each solution set separately, then find their INTERSECTION (the values satisfying BOTH).',
'explanation', 3),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
'Worked Examples — Step by Step',
E'## LEVEL 1: Basic Linear Equations\n\n### Example 1.1\n**Solve: 4x + 9 = 25**\n\n4x + 9 = 25\n4x = 25 - 9 (subtract 9 from both sides)\n4x = 16\nx = 4 (divide both sides by 4)\n\n**Check**: 4(4) + 9 = 16 + 9 = 25 ✓\n\n### Example 1.2\n**Solve: 5 - 3x = 14**\n\n5 - 3x = 14\n-3x = 14 - 5 (subtract 5 from both sides)\n-3x = 9\nx = -3 (divide both sides by -3)\n\n**Check**: 5 - 3(-3) = 5 + 9 = 14 ✓\n\n### Example 1.3 (With Brackets)\n**Solve: 3(2x - 1) = 4x + 9**\n\nStep 1 - Expand: 6x - 3 = 4x + 9\nStep 2 - Collect x terms: 6x - 4x = 9 + 3\nStep 3 - Simplify: 2x = 12\nStep 4 - Divide: x = 6\n\n**Check**: 3(12-1) = 33 and 4(6)+9 = 33 ✓\n\n### Example 1.4 (With Fractions)\n**Solve: (2x + 1)/3 = (x - 2)/2**\n\nLCM of 3 and 2 is 6. Multiply every term by 6:\n6(2x+1)/3 = 6(x-2)/2\n2(2x+1) = 3(x-2)\n4x + 2 = 3x - 6\n4x - 3x = -6 - 2\nx = -8\n\n**Check**: (2(-8)+1)/3 = -15/3 = -5 and (-8-2)/2 = -10/2 = -5 ✓\n\n---\n\n## LEVEL 2: Quadratic Equations\n\n### Example 2.1 — Factorisation (a = 1)\n**Solve: x² - 7x + 12 = 0**\n\nNeed two numbers that multiply to 12 and add to -7.\nThose numbers are: -3 and -4 (since -3 × -4 = 12 and -3 + -4 = -7)\n\n(x - 3)(x - 4) = 0\n\nEither: x - 3 = 0 → x = 3\nOr: x - 4 = 0 → x = 4\n\n**Solutions: x = 3 or x = 4**\n\n### Example 2.2 — Factorisation (a ≠ 1)\n**Solve: 2x² + 7x + 3 = 0**\n\na × c = 2 × 3 = 6. Need two numbers multiplying to 6 and adding to 7: they are 1 and 6.\n\nSplit middle term: 2x² + x + 6x + 3 = 0\nFactor in pairs: x(2x + 1) + 3(2x + 1) = 0\nFactor out: (x + 3)(2x + 1) = 0\n\nEither: x + 3 = 0 → x = -3\nOr: 2x + 1 = 0 → x = -1/2\n\n**Solutions: x = -3 or x = -1/2**\n\n### Example 2.3 — Quadratic Formula\n**Solve: 3x² - 5x - 2 = 0**\n\na = 3, b = -5, c = -2\n\nDiscriminant: D = (-5)² - 4(3)(-2) = 25 + 24 = 49\n\nx = (5 ± √49) / (2 × 3) = (5 ± 7) / 6\n\nx = (5 + 7)/6 = 12/6 = **2**\nOR x = (5 - 7)/6 = -2/6 = **-1/3**\n\n### Example 2.4 — Completing the Square (Exam Level)\n**Solve: x² + 6x - 7 = 0 by completing the square**\n\nx² + 6x = 7 (move constant)\nx² + 6x + 9 = 7 + 9 (add (6/2)² = 9 to both sides)\n(x + 3)² = 16\nx + 3 = ±4\n\nx = 4 - 3 = **1** OR x = -4 - 3 = **-7**\n\n---\n\n## LEVEL 3: Simultaneous Equations\n\n### Example 3.1 — Elimination\n**Solve: 3x + 2y = 16 and 5x - 2y = 8**\n\nNotice: the y-coefficients are +2 and -2 (opposite signs). ADD the equations:\n3x + 2y = 16\n5x - 2y = 8\n-----------\n8x = 24\nx = 3\n\nSubstitute x = 3 into equation 1:\n3(3) + 2y = 16\n9 + 2y = 16\n2y = 7\ny = 3.5\n\n**Solution: x = 3, y = 3.5**\n\n### Example 3.2 — Substitution (WASSCE Style)\n**Solve: 2x + y = 9 and x - 3y = 7**\n\nFrom equation 1: y = 9 - 2x ... (express y in terms of x)\n\nSubstitute into equation 2:\nx - 3(9 - 2x) = 7\nx - 27 + 6x = 7\n7x = 34\nx = 34/7... hmm, let us try from equation 2 instead.\n\nFrom equation 2: x = 7 + 3y\nSubstitute into equation 1:\n2(7 + 3y) + y = 9\n14 + 6y + y = 9\n7y = -5\ny = -5/7\n\nx = 7 + 3(-5/7) = 7 - 15/7 = 49/7 - 15/7 = 34/7\n\n**Solution: x = 34/7, y = -5/7**\n\n---\n\n## LEVEL 4: Inequalities (Exam Standard)\n\n### Example 4.1\n**Find the values of x satisfying: 3x - 5 > 7 and 2x + 1 ≤ 13**\n\nInequality 1: 3x - 5 > 7\n3x > 12\nx > 4\n\nInequality 2: 2x + 1 ≤ 13\n2x ≤ 12\nx ≤ 6\n\nCombined solution: **4 < x ≤ 6**\n(x must be greater than 4 AND at most 6)\n\n### Example 4.2 — Word Problem\n**Twice a number decreased by 7 is not less than 15. Find the possible values.**\n\nLet the number = n\n2n - 7 ≥ 15\n2n ≥ 22\nn ≥ 11\n\n**The number is 11 or any value greater than 11.**',
'examples', 4),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000001',
'Common Mistakes + Practice Questions + Full Solutions',
E'## The 8 Most Dangerous Mistakes in Algebra\n\n### Mistake 1: Distributing to Only One Term\n- WRONG: `3(x + 4) = 3x + 4`\n- CORRECT: `3(x + 4) = 3x + 12` (multiply 3 by BOTH x and 4)\n\n### Mistake 2: Not Reversing Inequality When Dividing by Negative\n- WRONG: `-4x > 20` gives x > -5\n- CORRECT: `-4x > 20` gives x < -5 (divide by -4, reverse sign)\n\n### Mistake 3: Forgetting Both Roots of a Quadratic\n- Quadratic equations almost always have TWO roots. Find both.\n- When using quadratic formula, calculate BOTH the + and the - case.\n\n### Mistake 4: Sign Errors When Collecting Terms\n- WRONG: `5x - 3 = 2x + 7` becomes `5x - 2x = 7 - 3` → `3x = 4` ✓ (this is correct)\n- But `5x - 3 = 2x - 7` becomes `5x - 2x = -7 + 3` → `3x = -4` (remember to change sign when moving)\n\n### Mistake 5: Incorrect Verification\n- Always substitute your answer into the ORIGINAL equation, not a rearranged form.\n\n### Mistake 6: Algebraic Fraction Errors\n- WRONG: `x/3 + x/4 = x/7`\n- CORRECT: `x/3 + x/4 = 4x/12 + 3x/12 = 7x/12` (find common denominator)\n\n### Mistake 7: Simultaneous Equations — Not Substituting into the OTHER Equation\n- After finding one variable, always substitute into one of the ORIGINAL equations to find the second.\n\n### Mistake 8: Thinking x² = 9 has only one answer\n- x² = 9 means x = +3 OR x = -3 (both values squared give 9)\n\n---\n\n## Practice Questions\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice (Objective)\n\n**Question 1:** Solve: 3x - 4 = 2x + 7\n(A) x = 3   (B) x = 11   (C) x = -11   (D) x = 1\n\n**Question 2:** The roots of x² - x - 12 = 0 are:\n(A) 3 and -4   (B) -3 and 4   (C) 6 and -2   (D) 4 and 3\n\n**Question 3:** If 2x + y = 8 and x - y = 1, find x.\n(A) 2   (B) 3   (C) 4   (D) 5\n\n**Question 4:** For which values of x is 5 - 2x > 1?\n(A) x > 2   (B) x < 2   (C) x > -2   (D) x < -2\n\n**Question 5:** The discriminant of 2x² - 3x + 5 = 0 is:\n(A) 49   (B) -31   (C) 31   (D) -49\n\n### Section B: Theory Questions\n\n**Question 6:** Solve by completing the square: x² - 8x + 7 = 0\n\n**Question 7:** Solve simultaneously: 4x + 3y = 18 and 2x - y = 4\n\n**Question 8:** Find all values of x satisfying: -3 ≤ 2x + 1 ≤ 9. Represent on a number line.\n\n### Section C: Exam-Standard Questions\n\n**Question 9:** The length of a rectangle is 5 more than twice its width. If the area is 75 cm², form a quadratic equation and solve it to find the dimensions.\n\n**Question 10:** Find the range of values of k for which kx² - 4x + k = 0 has real roots.\n\n---\n\n## Full Solutions\n\n**Q1:** 3x - 4 = 2x + 7 → x = 11 → **(B)**\n\n**Q2:** x² - x - 12 = (x - 4)(x + 3) = 0 → x = 4 or x = -3 → **(B)**\n\n**Q3:** Add equations: 3x = 9 → x = 3 → **(B)**\n\n**Q4:** 5 - 2x > 1 → -2x > -4 → x < 2 (reverse sign) → **(B)**\n\n**Q5:** D = 9 - 4(2)(5) = 9 - 40 = -31 → **(B)**\n\n**Q6:** x² - 8x = -7 → x² - 8x + 16 = -7 + 16 = 9 → (x-4)² = 9 → x-4 = ±3 → x = 7 or x = 1\n\n**Q7:** From eq 2: y = 2x - 4. Substitute: 4x + 3(2x-4) = 18 → 10x - 12 = 18 → x = 3, y = 2\n\n**Q8:** -3 ≤ 2x + 1 ≤ 9 → -4 ≤ 2x ≤ 8 → **-2 ≤ x ≤ 4**\n\n**Q9:** Width = w, Length = 2w + 5. Area = w(2w+5) = 75. 2w² + 5w - 75 = 0. (2w + 15)(w - 5) = 0. w = 5 (reject w = -7.5). Width = 5 cm, Length = 15 cm.\n\n**Q10:** For real roots: D ≥ 0. D = 16 - 4(k)(k) = 16 - 4k². 16 - 4k² ≥ 0 → k² ≤ 4 → **-2 ≤ k ≤ 2** (and k ≠ 0)',
'practice', 5);
